Texy Syntax
***********

.[perex]
Texy syntax is a richer alternative to Markdown. The basics are just as simple, but on top of that you can write images with captions and alignment, tables with merged cells, or a class and style on any element, all without a single HTML tag. This page covers everything you can write in it.

Prefer writing in Markdown? It is described on the [Markdown Syntax |markdown] page.

Each syntax is listed with its ID, which a programmer uses to [turn it on or off |configuration#Which Syntaxes Are Enabled] when it is not wanted on the site or when it is not enabled by default.


Cheat Sheet
===========

| [#Text Formatting]                   |  Syntax
|-----------------------------------------------
| [Bold text |#Text Formatting]        | .[text-code] ''**bold text**''
| [Italics  |#Text Formatting]         | ''*italics*'' or ''//italics//''
| [Inline code  |#Text Formatting]     | ''`code`''
| [#Links]                             | ''"text":URL'' or ''[text](URL)''
| [#Images]                            | ''[* image.jpg *]''
| [#Disabling Formatting]              | &apos;&apos;special characters&apos;&apos;
|-----------------------------------------------
| Elements
|-----------------------------------------------
| [#Underlined Headings]               | H1 <br> ===
| [#Surrounded Headings]               | ''### H1'' <br> ## H2
| [#Bulleted Lists]                    | ''- first'' <br> ''- second''
| [#Numbered Lists]                    | ''1) first'' <br> ''2) second''
| [#Definition Lists]                  | term: <br> &nbsp; ''- first''
| [#Blockquotes]                       | ''> blockquote''
| [#Horizontal Rules]                  | ''---''
| [#Tables]                            | ''\| cell \| cell \|''
| [Code blocks|#Preformatted Text]     | ''/--'' <br> ... <br> ''\--''
|-----------------------------------------------
| [#Modifiers]
|--------------------------------------------------------
| title                                | ''.(title)''
| CSS class                            | ''.[btn btn-primary]''
| ID                                   | ''.[#id]''
| CSS style or HTML attribute          | ''.{color: blue}'' or ''.{target: _blank}''
| horizontal alignment                 | ''.< .> .<> .=''
| vertical alignment                   | ''.^ .- ._''


Paragraphs
==========

Texy treats one or more consecutive lines of text as a paragraph. As soon as you leave **one blank line** between them, Texy automatically knows that a new paragraph begins.

This means Texy joins lines that belong together. So you don't have to worry about a sentence breaking in the middle when you make your editor window narrower.

```texy
This is the first paragraph. It can easily span several lines
and Texy joins them into one continuous block of text.

Only here, after a blank line, does a brand new, second paragraph begin.
```

You can, however, turn line joining off in the configuration, and then every line break in the source is kept:

```php
$texy->config->mergeLines = false;
```


Line Breaks
-----------

But what if you only need to start a new line without creating a whole new paragraph? This typically comes in handy for poems, song lyrics or addresses. **Start the new line with a single space**.

```texy
John Smith,
 5 Quiet Lane
 London SW1A 1AA
```


Styling Paragraphs
------------------

Sometimes you need a whole paragraph to stand out: to make it the lead paragraph of an article, center it, or style it as a box. That is what [#modifiers] are for. You can place them either on a separate line **before** the paragraph or at the end of its last line.

```texy
.[perex]
This is the introductory paragraph of the article; thanks to the modifier
it gets the CSS class "perex" and can look different from the rest of the text.

This paragraph, in turn, has a unique ID. .[#intro-section]

And this paragraph will be centered. .<>
```


Text Formatting
===============

| syntax                             |  output                     | Syntax ID
|-----------------------------------------------------------------------------
| .[text-code] ''**bold text**''     | **bold text**               | `phrase/strong`
| ''*italics* or //italics//''       | *italics*                   | `phrase/em-alt`, `phrase/em-alt2`, `phrase/em`
| ''***bold italics***''             | ***bold italics***          | `phrase/strong+em`
| ''`inline code`''                  | `inline code`               | `phrase/code`
| ''x^2 … O_2''                      | x^2 … O_2                   | `phrase/sup-alt`, `phrase/sub-alt`
| ''x^^2^^ … O__2__''                | x^2 … O_2                   | `phrase/sup`🔸, `phrase/sub`🔸
| ''++inserted text++''              | <ins>inserted text</ins>    | `phrase/ins`🔸
| ''--deleted text--''               | <del>deleted text</del>     | `phrase/del`🔸
| ''>>quoted text<<''                | >>quoted text<<             | `phrase/quote`
| ''"blue text .{color: blue}"''     | "blue text .{color: blue}"  | `phrase/span`
| ''~blue text .{color: blue}~''     | ~blue text .{color: blue}~  | `phrase/span-alt`
| ''"et al."((and others))''         | "et al."((and others))      | `phrase/acronym`
| ''NBA((National Basketball Association))'' | NBA((National Basketball Association)) | `phrase/acronym-alt`

The syntaxes marked 🔸 are not allowed by default and you have to turn them on. For example:

```php
$texy->allowedSyntax['phrase/ins'] = true;
```

For simple numeric superscripts and subscripts you can use the short syntax `x^2` and `O_2`, but for more complex cases the variant with doubled characters is more robust, or you can use the HTML tags `<sup>` and `<sub>`.

There **must be no spaces** right inside the marks:

```texy
Wrong: ** this will not be bold **
Right: **this will be bold**
```


Styling Text
------------

This is one of the most powerful features of Texy. You can "attach" [#modifiers] to any formatted text and give it a CSS class, an ID or an inline style. The modifier always goes **right before the closing mark**:

```texy
This text is **strong and green .{color:green}** like the Hulk.

Warning: //This feature is deprecated .[deprecated]//
```

If you want to apply a modifier to text without making it bold or italic, wrap the text in quotes `"` or tildes `~`. Texy turns that into the universal HTML tag `<span>` with your styles:

```texy
Ordinary text, but "this bit is red .{color: red}", and the rest is not.
```


Formatted Text as a Link
------------------------

You can turn formatted text into a link by simply adding a colon and a URL:

```texy
Visit our **new gallery**:https://example.com/gallery
```

This works for bold text, italics and inline code alike.


Writing Special Characters
--------------------------

What if you want to write `**text**` literally, asterisks included, without it turning into bold text? You have three options:

- a backslash is the quickest way to "neutralize" an asterisk `\**text\**`, but before other characters it does nothing and stays in the text
- double apostrophes [turn Texy off|#Disabling Formatting] for the whole phrase `''**text**''`
- you can use standard HTML entities `&ast;&ast;text&ast;&ast;`


Links
=====

Links are the soul of the internet. Texy is designed to make writing them as natural and readable as possible, right in the text.

The basic link syntax is simple and very readable. Wrap the link text in `"` (or other characters for [#text formatting]) and attach a colon and the target URL right after it:

```texy
Visit the official website of "Nette Framework":https://nette.org.

If you have a question, "write to us":info@example.com.
```

The nice thing is that Texy is smart enough to work out where the URL ends. So you don't have to worry about it accidentally pulling a period or comma at the end of a sentence into the link. But if the URL contains unusual characters, you can wrap it in square brackets to say exactly where the address starts and ends:

```texy
"Read our article":[https://example.com/news?id=1&category=articles]
```

Syntax IDs `phrase/span`, `phrase/span-alt`, `phrase/link`


Alternative Link Syntax
-----------------------

Are you used to the link format of Markdown or Wikipedia? Texy understands those too. Pick the style that suits you best.

```texy
[Link text](https://example.com)         // Markdown style
[Link text | https://example.com]        // MediaWiki style
text:[target URL or reference]           // Single-word link
```

Syntax IDs `phrase/markdown`, `phrase/wikilink`, `phrase/quicklink`, `phrase/link`


Keeping Things Tidy with References
-----------------------------------

In longer texts it can be awkward to put long URLs straight into paragraphs: they make the text harder to read and follow. For these cases Texy has **reference links**.

In the text you use only a short, easy-to-remember reference name. Then, at the end of the document, you define all these references neatly in one place.

```texy
We recommend studying "the official documentation":[doc] and going through "the syntax examples":[syntax].
The whole project is built on "Nette":[nette].

​[doc]: https://texy.deegee.dev
​[syntax]: https://texy.deegee.dev/syntax
​[nette]: https://nette.org
```

A link can also be just the reference name in square brackets, `[nette]`. This syntax is off by default, though, because it is easily confused with ordinary text in brackets. A programmer can turn it on with the ID `link/reference`.

Syntax IDs `link/reference`, `link/definition`


Automatic Links
---------------

Whenever you write a URL (starting with `http://`, `https://`, `ftp://`, `www.`) or an email address in the text, Texy recognizes it automatically and turns it into a clickable link. You don't have to do anything at all.

```texy
You can find our website at www.example.com.
For support, write to support@example.com.
```

Syntax IDs `link/url`, `link/email`


Styling Links
-------------

With [#modifiers] you can easily add further properties to links:

```texy
"External link .[external](Opens in a new window){target:_blank}":https://google.com
```

The special class `nofollow` adds the attribute `rel="nofollow"` to the link, which tells search engines not to follow it. This is handy for links in comments, for example.

```texy
"A link I don't trust .[nofollow]":https://example.com
```


Automatic Email Obfuscation
---------------------------

Texy automatically obfuscates (masks) email addresses to hide them from spambots:

```html
<a href="mailto:info&#64;example.com">info&#64;<!-- -->example.com</a>
```

You can turn this behavior off:

```php
$texy->htmlOutput->obfuscateEmail = false;
```


Raw HTML
========

Texy is designed so that you never have to write HTML. But what if you run into a situation where inserting an HTML tag directly is simpler, or you need to build something Texy syntax can't express? No problem. Texy gives you complete freedom to combine both worlds.

You can switch freely between Texy syntax and plain HTML whenever it suits you.

```texy
This is **bold text** in Texy and this is <strong>bold text</strong> in HTML.

<div class="info-box">
	<h3>You can insert whole complex blocks too</h3>
</div>
```

You may be thinking that inserting raw HTML could be risky. What if you make a mistake, or someone inserts malicious code? Texy has that covered:

- **It fixes mistakes:** When you forget to close a tag or close tags in the wrong order, Texy fixes it, so the resulting HTML is always properly closed and won't break your page.
- **It controls what gets through:** A programmer can decide which HTML tags and which of their attributes are allowed on the site. A tag that is not allowed is shown as plain text, and a disallowed attribute, such as `onclick`, disappears. By default, common HTML tags are allowed with all their attributes.
- **It protects comments:** For text from visitors, Texy has a safe mode that lets only a small set of harmless tags through, protecting the site against XSS attacks.

This gives you full control over which HTML, say, your editors can use. How to set the allowed tags is described in the [configuration |configuration#Allowed HTML Tags, Classes and Styles].

Put a script (`<script>`) inside a [`/--html` |#Disabling Formatting] block. In ordinary text its lines would merge into one and a `//` comment would swallow the rest of the code.

Syntax IDs `html/tag`, `html/comment`, `html/entity`


Headings
========

Texy offers you two elegant and intuitive ways to create headings: **underlined** and **surrounded**.


Underlined Headings
-------------------

This style is reminiscent of a typewriter. Simply put an underline (at least 3 characters) below the heading. The underline character decides how important the heading is. From most to least important, they are: `#` `*` `=` `-`

```texy
This is the most important heading in the document
​##################################################

And this is a second-level heading
​**********************************
```

Syntax ID `heading/underlined`


Surrounded Headings
-------------------

This way is very quick to type. You "wrap" the heading text in `#` or `=` characters. Here the level of the heading is decided by the **number** of characters used (2 to 7). The more characters, the more important the heading.

```texy
==== The most important heading (H1)

=== Less important (H2)

== Even less important (H3)
```

You can use the marks on both sides (for better visual clarity) or only at the start. Texy handles both variants.

The levels H1, H2, H3 in the examples are not fixed, though. Texy balances the headings in an article, so the most important of them gets the highest level, no matter how the author wrote it. How this works is described in the [configuration |configuration#Heading Balancing].

Syntax ID `heading/surrounded`


Styling Headings
----------------

You can add [#modifiers] to any heading. This lets you give it a specific CSS class for styling, or a unique ID you can then link to.

```texy
A red heading .[red-heading]
​============================

### Heading with a unique ID for linking .[#contact]
```


Automatic Anchors for Easy Navigation
-------------------------------------

Don't want to make up an ID for every heading by hand? Texy can do it for you! In the configuration you can turn on automatic ID generation for all headings. This is incredibly useful for linking directly to particular sections.

```php
// Enable automatic ID generation
$texy->config->headingIds = true;

// Optionally change the prefix of generated IDs, the default is 'toc-'
$texy->config->headingIdPrefix = 'section-';
```

With this setting, the heading `## My Chapter` automatically gets an ID such as `id="section-my-chapter"`, without you having to write anything extra.


Lists
=====


Bulleted Lists
--------------

For a quick list of items where order doesn't matter, a bulleted list is a great fit. Just start each line with a dash `-`, an asterisk `*` or a plus sign `+`, followed by a space. All three characters work the same, so you can pick whichever you like best. Just don't mix them within one list: a different character starts a new list.

```texy
What we need to buy:

- Milk
- Bread
- Eggs
```

Syntax ID `list`


Numbered Lists
--------------

Texy supports various numbering styles:

| `1.`    | Arabic numerals (with a period)
| `1)`    | Arabic numerals (with a parenthesis)
| `a)`    | Lowercase letters
| `A)`    | Uppercase letters
| `I.`    | Roman numerals (with a period)
| `I)`    | Roman numerals (with a parenthesis)

The magic is that you don't have to worry about getting the numbering right at all. Even if you number every item with a 1, Texy renumbers them for you automatically. That is a huge advantage when you later need to add, delete or move an item.

A list with a period (`1.` and `I.`) must start with 1 and have at least two items; otherwise it stays an ordinary paragraph. A list with a parenthesis can also start with another number or letter, such as `3)` or `c)`, and the numbering then continues from it.

Syntax IDs `list/decimal`, `list/roman`, `list/alpha`


Nested and Combined Lists
-------------------------

Lists really show their strength once you start combining and nesting them. That way you can build clear, multi-level structures. To nest a line, simply indent it with spaces or a tab.

```texy
1) First chapter
	a) Section 1.1
		- First point
		- Second point
	b) Section 1.2
2) Second chapter
	- Main idea
	- Another note
```


Definition Lists
----------------

When you need a glossary or a clear explanation of several terms, a definition list is ideal.

On the first line, write the term you want to define and end it with a colon. On the following lines write its definition, indenting each line and starting it with a dash `-`.

```texy
HTML:
  - A markup language for building web pages.
  - Short for HyperText Markup Language.

CSS:
  - A language describing how pages are displayed (styled).
  - Short for Cascading Style Sheets.
```

Syntax ID `list/definition`


Styling Lists
-------------

As with other elements in Texy, you can easily add [#modifiers] to lists to change how they look.

**The whole list:** Write the modifier on the line **before** the list starts.

```texy
.[colorful-list]
- First item
- Second item
```

**A single item:** Add the modifier at the **end** of the line of the item or definition term.

```texy
- Ordinary item
- This item is important! .{font-weight: bold}
- Another ordinary item
```


Images
======

The basic syntax is very simple. Just wrap the path to the image (whether a local file or a URL) in square brackets and asterisks:

```texy
[* image.jpg *]
[* https://example.com/logo.png *]
```

You will often want text to flow around an image. That is what the simple alignment marks are for; they go before the closing bracket:

```texy
[* image.jpg <] This text will flow smoothly around the image on its right side.

[* image.jpg >] Here, on the other hand, the text will flow around the image on its left side.

[* large-image.jpg *]
This text will continue below the image, without flowing around it.
```

A properly inserted image should also have "alternative text", which is shown when the image doesn't load. With a [modifier|#modifiers] you can add this text as well as other styling options.

```texy
[* landscape-photo.jpg .(A beautiful mountain landscape at sunset)[main-photo] *]
```

Texy wraps an image that stands alone on a line in `<figure>`, just like an image with a [caption |#Visible Caption Under an Image]. A modifier written after it on the same line, such as `[* photo.jpg *] .[gallery]`, belongs to the `<figure>`.

Syntax IDs `image`, `figure`


Image Dimensions
----------------

For local images, Texy can find out their dimensions automatically (if the programmer sets the path `$texy->htmlOutput->imageBasePath`) and add them to the HTML, so the browser can lay out the page before the images load. But if you want to set the dimensions by hand, you have several options:

| `[* img.jpg 150x100 *]` | Exact width of 150px and height of 100px
| `[* img.jpg 150x? *]`   | Width of 150px; the browser computes the height, keeping the aspect ratio
| `[* img.jpg ?x100 *]`   | Height of 100px; the browser computes the width


Clickable Images
----------------

Want a large image to appear when someone clicks a small thumbnail? Or an image to link to another page? Just add a colon and the target URL after the image syntax.

```texy
[* thumbnail.jpg *]:large.jpg
[* nette-logo.png *]:https://nette.org
```

For galleries there is also a handy shortcut, `::`. It turns the image into a link to the same file, so `[* photo.jpg *]::` opens the image itself when clicked.

The older way of writing a link to a large image inside the brackets, `[* small.jpg | large.jpg *]`, is deprecated: Texy 4 renders only the small image from it and reports an `E_USER_DEPRECATED` warning to the programmer. Use `[* small.jpg *]:large.jpg` instead.


Visible Caption Under an Image
------------------------------

If you want to add a visible caption under an image (such as the author's name or a description of the scene), write three asterisks `***` and the caption text after it. Texy turns this into semantic HTML5: the image in `<figure>` and the caption in `<figcaption>`. A programmer can choose different markup in the [configuration |configuration#Images].

```texy
[* photo.jpg *] *** This is a caption. It can contain **more formatting** too.
```


Image References
----------------

If you use one image several times in the text, or want to keep all image definitions neatly in one place, you can use references. In the text you use only a placeholder name, and at the end of the file you define what this name stands for.

```texy
Our logo [* company-logo *] shows the symbol of our vision.

​[* company-logo *]: /images/logo.svg 200x50 .(Our company logo)
```

This approach makes the main text much clearer and makes images easier to manage.

Syntax ID `image/definition`


Preformatted Text
=================

In Texy you can easily insert blocks of code or any preformatted text that you want displayed exactly as you write it, including all spaces and line endings. That is ideal for source code samples, logs or ASCII art.

To insert such a block, surround it with `/--` and `\--`:

```texy
/--
function hello() {
	echo 'Hello World';
}
\--
```

Texy processes nothing inside such a block, neither its own marks nor HTML, and outputs it as `<pre>`.

To make your code even more readable, you can tell Texy which programming language it is written in. The website can then highlight its syntax, as the [Customizing Elements |custom-handlers] page shows. Just add the keyword `code` and the name of the language after the opening `/--`:

```texy
/--code javascript
console.log('JavaScript');
\--
```

The language name alone, such as `/--php`, is not enough. A block of a type Texy doesn't know is shown as written, including the lines `/--php` and `\--`. A programmer can, however, handle such a type, as described in [Adding Custom Syntax |custom-syntax#A New Block Type in Texy Syntax].

Texy also understands code blocks as you know them from Markdown, between lines of three backticks. After the opening backticks you can add a language and a [modifier |#Modifiers]:

```
 ```php .[example]
 $texy = new Texy\Texy;
 ```
```

Nothing inside a code block gets formatted, and tabs and spaces stay exactly as you wrote them.

A `/--pre` block is preformatted text in which spaces and line endings are preserved, but HTML tags still apply. Texy marks are not processed in it:

```texy
/--pre
Name:     <b>Jane</b>
Phone:    776 552 046
\--
```

Syntax IDs `blocks`, `block/default`, `block/code`, `block/pre`, `fenced-code`


Content Blocks (Divs)
=====================

Texy lets you create generic `<div>` blocks, so you can easily group content into logical units and then style them:

You create a block with the marks `/--div` and `\--`. On top of that, you can easily add [#modifiers]:

```texy
/--div .[important]
## Important Notice

This text will be enclosed in a `<div class="important">` block.
Thanks to that, you can style it with CSS to make it stand out.
\--
```

Another strength of `<div>` blocks is that you can nest them. That way you can build even more complex structures right in Texy, without writing HTML by hand.

```texy
/--div .[outer]
	This is the outer block.

	/--div .[inner]
		And this is the nested, inner block.
	\--

	Here we are back in the outer block.
\--
```
With this simple syntax you can keep your content clear and semantically well structured.

Syntax ID `block/div`


Disabling Formatting
====================

Sometimes it is handy to "turn off" Texy for a moment and insert a piece of text in which Texy should not process its marks.

If you need to insert a more complex HTML structure without Texy parsing its marks, use a `/--html` block:

```texy
/--html
<em>This text will be processed as HTML, so it will be in italics.</em>

**But Texy ignores these asterisks, so this will not be bold.**
\--
```

If you want to display text exactly as it is written and ignore all marks (both Texy and HTML), use a `/--text` block. Everything inside it is shown as plain text.

```texy
/--text
<em>This text is shown with its tags, but it will not be in italics.</em>

**This will not be bold either.**
\--
```

But what if you don't want to turn Texy off for a whole block of text, only for a short phrase in the middle of a sentence? For these cases there is an elegant and quick solution: wrap the text in **double apostrophes** `''`:

```texy
If you want to show how to write bold text, you write: The syntax is ''**bold text**''.
```

The result will not be bold text; the string `**bold text**` is printed literally.

And text that doesn't belong in the output at all, such as a note for other authors, goes into a `/--comment` block. Texy leaves it out completely.

Syntax IDs `block/html`, `block/text`, `block/comment`, `phrase/notexy`


Tables
======

To create a table, start each line with the character `|` and separate the individual cells with it as well. Texy takes care of the alignment and the correct HTML.

```texy
| John | Smith | London
| Eve  | Brown | Bristol
```

The result is a clear, properly formatted table.

Syntax ID `table`


Table Header
------------

Every good table should have a header that describes what each column contains. You create the header by separating it from the rest of the table with a line of dashes `-`.

```texy
| Name     | Age | City
|----------|-----|--------
| John     | 25  | London
| Eve      | 30  | Bristol
```

A table can also have a caption. Write it on the first line after `|##`:

```texy
|## Drying time
| Fruit   | Time
|---------|-----
| plums   | 12 h
```

Alternatively, you can define headers for individual rows (for example when the first column holds labels). You do that by adding an asterisk `*` right after the opening `|`.

```texy
|* Name    | John   | Eve
|* Age     | 25     | 30
|* City    | London | Bristol
```


Merging Cells
-------------

Sometimes you need to join several cells together, whether across columns or rows.

**Merging columns:** To join cells horizontally, leave a cell empty, so that you get a doubled vertical bar `||`. The empty cell then merges with the cell to its left.

```texy
| Name            || Age
|----------------------------
| John | Smith     | 25
```

**Merging rows:** To join cells vertically, put a caret `^` in the cell you want to attach to the one above it. It tells Texy: "Merge this cell with the one above."

```texy
| Month    | Sales    |
|----------|-----------
| January  | 150 pcs  |
| February |         ^|
| March    | 210 pcs  |
```

In this example, the January and February sales cells are merged into one.

This is how you merge several cells across rows and columns:

```texy
| First Name | Last Name | Age
|----------------------------
| Bill                  || 50
|                       ^| 52
| Jim        | Beam      | 70
```


Styling Tables
--------------

As with other elements in Texy, you can add [#modifiers] to tables and their parts to change how they look (e.g. CSS classes, styles or IDs).

**The whole table:** Place the modifier for the whole table on a separate line right before it.

```texy
.[data-table table-striped]
| Header 1   | Header 2
|------------|------------
| data       | data
```

**Individual rows:** To style a particular row, add the modifier to its end.

```texy
| Name  | Status
|-------|--------------
| Peter | Approved
| Jane  | Rejected     | .{background: #ffdddd}
```

**Individual columns:** To style a whole column, put the modifier at the start of the first cell of that column.

```texy
| Name           | .>  Price | In Stock
|----------------|-----------|---------
| Product A      | $1,200    | Yes
| Product B      | $850      | No
```

**A specific cell:** Write the modifier for a single cell right into it, usually at the end of its content.

```texy
| Task                 | Status
|----------------------|-------------------------------------
| Prepare materials    | Done
| Check the data       | In progress .{color: orange; font-weight: bold}
```


Blockquotes
===========

When you need to highlight someone else's thought in your text, quote a source, or just visually set a block of text apart, simply start the line with the character `>`.

```texy
> This is a quote. It serves to highlight an important thought or an excerpt from another source.
```

A quote doesn't have to be a single paragraph. If you want to continue with another paragraph within the same quote, simply insert a line containing only `>`.

```texy
> This is the first paragraph of the quote. Lorem ipsum dolor sit amet.
>
> And this is the second paragraph, which still belongs to the same quote.
> This way you can structure longer texts too.
```

Texy even supports nested quotes, which comes in handy when you quote someone who is quoting someone else. For each further level of nesting, add another `>` character.

```texy
> This is the outer, main quote.
>
> > And this is a nested quote at the second level.
>
> Here the text returns to the main quote.
```

Inside quotes you can of course use other formatting too, such as **bold text** or *italics*.

Syntax ID `blockquote`


Horizontal Rules
================

Sometimes you need to divide text visually. A horizontal rule is perfect for that. On a separate line, write three or more dashes `---` or asterisks `***`.

```texy
The first part of the text on some topic.

***

The second part of the text, which begins after the visual break.
```

A horizontal rule **must be preceded by a blank line**. If you wrote it right below text, Texy would think you want to create an underlined heading.

Syntax ID `horizline`


Typography
==========

The power of Texy lies not only in formatting, but also in automatic typographic corrections. Texy takes care of the details that make text look professional and read well, all according to the typographic rules of the language you set. You can focus on the content alone.

**Quotes:** You don't have to work out how to type proper typographic quotes on your keyboard. Texy does it for you.

It turns plain ''"typewriter quotes"'' into proper English “quotes” and nested ‘quotes’, or Czech „quotes“ and ‚quotes‘. The kind of quotes depends on the locale setting:

```php
$texy->config->locale = 'cs'; // Czech
$texy->config->locale = 'en'; // English
```

**Dashes and hyphens:** Texy recognizes when to use a short hyphen (in compound words) and when a longer dash, for example in ranges (10-15) or between words.

```texy
10-15           → 10–15             (en dash for ranges)
well-known      → well-known        (the hyphen stays)
word - word     → word&nbsp;– word  (en dash between words)
word -- word    → word&nbsp;– word  (en dash between words)
word --- word   → word&nbsp;— word  (em dash)
```

**Non-breaking spaces**: One of the biggest advantages is that Texy automatically inserts non-breaking spaces where they are needed. With `locale = 'cs'` this keeps one-letter words (such as `k`, `s`, `v`, `z`) from being left alone at the end of a line, a common typographic error in Czech, Slovak and Polish.

```texy
// You write (Czech for "I visited the castle in Prague"):
Navštívil jsem hrad v Praze.

// Texy makes sure "v" never stays at the end of a line:
Navštívil jsem hrad v&nbsp;Praze.
```

In the same way it takes care of proper spaces in phone numbers and dates, so they don't get split across lines.

```texy
+420 776 552 046 → +420&nbsp;776&nbsp;552&nbsp;046  (all spaces non-breaking)
```

**Automatic symbols:** Texy also makes it easier to write commonly used symbols.

| You write | Texy generates | Description
|-----
| `...`    | …               | Ellipsis
| `(c)`    | ©               | Copyright
| `(r)`    | ®               | Registered trademark
| `(tm)`   | ™               | Trademark
| `10 x 5` | 10 × 5          | Multiplication sign
| `+-`     | ±               | Plus-minus
| `<-` `->` `<->`  | ← → ↔   | Arrows (surround them with spaces)

Thanks to these automatic adjustments, your text always looks professional, without you having to know complicated keyboard shortcuts or HTML entities.


Hyphenating Long Words
----------------------

You know the problem: a long word such as the Czech "nejneobhospodařovávatelnějšími" turns up in the text and breaks the whole page layout on the narrow screen of a mobile phone. Fortunately, Texy offers an elegant solution: it can insert invisible "soft hyphens" (`&shy;`) into the word. These tell the browser where (between syllables) it can safely break the word if it doesn't fit at the end of a line. If the whole word fits on the line, the soft hyphens stay hidden and nothing happens.

```html
nejneobhospoda&shy;řovávatelnější&shy;mi
```

Thanks to this, your text always adapts nicely to any screen width without unwanted horizontal scrolling.

Hyphenation is on by default for words of 20 characters or more. It follows Czech syllables, so it applies to text in Czech and Slovak. A programmer can change the length or turn hyphenation off:

```php
$texy->config->hyphenationWordLimit = 25;
$texy->config->hyphenation = false;
```


Emoticons
=========

Texy can automatically turn classic text smileys into graphical emoticons. Just type a smiley the way you are used to, and Texy takes care of the rest.

| You write | Texy generates
|-----
| `:-)`    | 🙂
| `:-(`    | ☹
| `;-)`    | 😉
| `:-D`    | 😁
| `:-P`    | 😛

To prevent unwanted conversions, in technical texts for example, this feature is off by default. If you want to use it, simply enable it:

```php
$texy->allowedSyntax['emoticon'] = true;
```

The [configuration |configuration#Emoticons] page describes the full set of emoticons and how to add your own.

Syntax ID `emoticon`


Modifiers
=========

A modifier adds a CSS class, an ID, a style, an HTML attribute, a title or alignment to an element. It starts with a period, and its parts are recognized by their brackets:

| syntax                                    | meaning
|-----------------------------------------------------------
| ''.(title)''                              | title; for an image, a description for those who cannot see it
| ''.[btn btn-primary]''                    | CSS classes
| ''.[#contact]''                           | ID
| ''.{color: blue}''                        | CSS style
| ''.{target: _blank}''                     | HTML attribute
| ''.<'' ''.>'' ''.<>'' ''.=''              | alignment left, right, center, justify
| ''.^'' ''.-'' ''._''                      | vertical alignment top, middle, bottom

You can combine the parts freely in one modifier, such as `.(Important)[box #notice]{border: 1px solid}<>`. An image is aligned differently, with the mark `<]` or `>]` at the end, and alignment in its modifier would be read as that mark.

Where to write a modifier depends on the element:

- **paragraph, heading, list item, table row:** at the end of the line; for a paragraph also on a separate line before it
- **whole list, table:** on a separate line before it
- **formatted text and link:** right before the closing mark, for example `**text .[class]**` or `"link .[class]":url`
- **image:** before the closing `*]`; for an image alone on a line also after it, where it then belongs to the `<figure>`
- **blockquote:** on a separate line before it
- **horizontal rule:** after it, for example `--- .[class]`
- **table cell and column:** as shown in the [#Tables] chapter
- **block:** after the opening mark, for example `/--div .[class]`

The section on each element on this page shows examples. Which classes and styles authors may use is decided by the programmer in the [configuration |configuration#Allowed HTML Tags, Classes and Styles].

